president Trump has lit the bureaucratic  fuse for his new Space Command but the  idea comes with a hefty price tag and  plenty of critics national security  correspondent Jennifer Griffin looks at  both sides tonight from the Pentagon  this space command will be the 11th  combatant command in the US military the  announcement was timed to coincide with  the launch of the spacex falcon 9 a  long-delayed 500 million-dollar gps  satellite for the US military but the  mission was aborted until Wednesday I  can assure you that space was a priority  of this president and of his vice  president the president's memo  establishing the command is an effort to  consolidate the military space  operations and defend US assets in space  satellites the u.s. relies on for  navigation communication and  surveillance the new command is expected  to cost about 800 million dollars over  the next five years as the president  said and as you all well know space is  in his words a war fighting domain China  has tested missiles designed to destroy  satellites China and Russia are working  to station new weapons directly in space  the Space Command is the first step  toward president Trump's space force  which at first the Pentagon resisted  that my direction the Pentagon is now  working to create the sixth branch of  the American armed forces called the  space force woods dog the Air Force  which oversees space for the military  initially opposed the president's idea  I'm absolutely in favor of a separate  space command to provide that war  fighting focus but I am not in favor of  a separate space force a separate  military service it's bureaucratically  complicated it will be expensive it's  not necessary the US military had a  Space Command from 1985 to 2002 but it  was disbanded after 9/11 so that  northern command could be set up to  defend the homeland breath Jennifer  Griffin at the Pentagon Jennifer thank  you
 

